When troubleshooting a Linux system, your diagnostic strategy should target specific subsystems: CPU, Memory, Storage/IO, and Network.
The most effective commands for performance monitoring and troubleshooting on Linux are categorized below by their primary function:
1. Central Resource Dashboards
These commands give you a quick, birds-eye view of how the entire system is performing.
• uptime: Shows how long the system has been running, active users, and load averages over the last 1, 5, and 15 minutes.
• top: The classic real-time process viewer.
	• Tip: Press Shift + P to sort by CPU usage, or Shift + M to sort by Memory.
• htop: A modern, interactive version of top featuring color-coded visual bars, process trees (F5), and quick process-killing (F9).
2. CPU & Process Monitoring
Use these when applications seem sluggish or are locking up the machine.
• ps aux: Captures a snapshot of all running processes, displaying details like Process ID (PID), user, %CPU, and %Memory.
	• Example: ps aux | grep nginx (Finds a specific service's process details).
• mpstat -P ALL 1: Breaks down CPU utilization per individual core every 1 second. Essential for spotting asymmetric loads.
• kill -9 <PID>: Forcefully terminates a rogue, un-killable process.
3. Memory Diagnostics
If you suspect memory leaks or the system is aggressively swapping, start here.
• free -h: Displays total, used, and available RAM and Swap memory in human-readable sizes (GB/MB).
• vmstat 1: Provides continuous summaries of virtual memory, paging, and CPU activity.
	• Look at: The si (swap in) and so (swap out) columns. High numbers mean the server is running out of physical RAM.
4. Storage & Disk I/O
High storage input/output can cripple application speeds, even if the CPU is mostly idle.
• df -h: Shows total capacity and available disk space across all mounted filesystems.
• du -sh /* | sort -h: Calculates and lists the total disk usage of files and folders, sorted by size. Great for finding hidden log files eating up space.
• iostat -xz 1: Generates extended reports for disk read/write throughput.
	• Look at: The %util column. If a disk is at 100%, it is saturated.
• iotop -o: Operates like top, but displays a real-time list of processes actively writing/reading to disk.
5. Network Troubleshooting
When services can’t connect internally or are failing to reach the open internet.
• ss -tunapl: Replaces the legacy netstat command. It lists all active TCP/UDP connections and reveals what ports are listening and which processes own them.
• ip a / ip route: Identifies your network interface configurations, IP addresses, and routing tables.
• ping <host>: Confirms basic point-to-point network connectivity and latency.
• traceroute <host>: Maps out every hop a packet takes to a destination, isolating where a connection breaks.
• tcpdump -i eth0: Captures and inspects raw network packets flowing through an interface.
6. System & Application Logs
When the hardware is fine but a service refuses to start.
• journalctl -xe: Fetches recent systemd service manager logs, appending structural errors at the bottom.
• journalctl -u <service_name> -f: Follows and streams logs for a specific service (e.g., nginx or docker) in real time.
• dmesg -T: Reads the kernel ring buffer to reveal hardware failures, drive disconnections, or Out-Of-Memory (OOM) kills.
• strace -p <PID>: Hooks onto a running process to trace its system calls, showing you exactly what system file it's failing to open.
Command Cheat Sheet Matrix
Symptom	Primary Command	Secondary Deep Dive
System is generally slow	top or htop	uptime
Out of Memory errors	free -h	vmstat 1
Slow database or file access	iostat -xz 1	iotop -o
"Disk full" errors	df -h	du -sh /* | sort -h
Application port unreachable	ss -tunapl	tcpdump
